#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tk-errors — what THIS session was refused, read from its own transcript.

WHY IT EXISTS: a refusal is the one event a session reliably forgets. The tool
call that came back `is_error` was answered three turns ago, the model moved on,
and at the close nobody re-reads the scrollback — so a `tk-queue add` a hook
turned away leaves no item, and the wrap-up reports a queue it believes it wrote.
Sibling of `tk-context`, and the same trick: the session's own jsonl holds the
record, and `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` names the file.

THREE SIGNAL CLASSES, and they answer different questions.

1. REFUSED CALLS — every `tool_result` carrying `is_error: true`. That is the
   harness's own verdict, not a guess: a permission denial, a hook exiting 2, a
   command that did not parse, a file that was not there. It is broad by design,
   because a session cannot know in advance which refusal mattered.

2. UNCONFIRMED QUEUE WRITES — a `tk-queue` subcommand that WRITES was invoked and
   the call printed no success line for it. This class exists because class 1
   does not cover it: a write can fail with the tool call reporting success (a
   refusal `tk-queue` prints on stdout and exits 0 on, a guard that answers in
   the model's voice, a call whose result never came back because the window
   ended). The queue is the one state whose loss is silent — the item simply is
   not there — so the invariant is checked rather than assumed.

3. COMMANDS NOT PARSED — a command carrying the binary's name that `shlex`
   refuses. Its own class, and advisory: what this reader knows about it is that
   it could not read it. Almost all of them are heredocs the shell ran without
   complaint — 128 of 135 rows over 254 transcripts, measured — because a
   heredoc's body is prose and an apostrophe in it is an unbalanced quote to
   `shlex`. Counting one as a write asserts an invocation nobody can show
   happened; the earlier version of this file guessed with a regex and read the
   English inside those heredocs as queue writes.

WHAT A SUCCESS LINE IS. Each writing subcommand prints one, and this file pins
its shape against `tk-queue`'s own `print` (`SUCCESS`, below). The pinning is
what makes the count honest and also what makes it brittle: reword a line in
`tk-queue` and this reader stops seeing it. `test_tk_errors.py` holds that seam
shut — it asserts every shape here is still a literal in `tk/bin/tk-queue`.

WHAT COUNTS AS AN INVOCATION. The command string is tokenized as the shell would
(`shlex`, comments honoured), and a token whose basename is `tk-queue` names one,
the subcommand being the first bare word after it — `--dir` and its value
skipped, as the parser skips them. A mention inside quotes never matches: the
shell keeps it in one token. Invocations are COUNTED, not merely found, and one
success line confirms one of them: a presence check let the first `added` line
answer for both halves of a batched command.

THE BLIND SPOT IN THAT COUNT: a loop. `for id in T1 T2 T3; do tk-queue bump
$id; done` is ONE invocation in the command text and three at runtime, so a
partial failure inside it reports nothing. The reader who batches a write in a
loop is the one who has to check the queue by hand.

NOT A GATE, A DIAGNOSIS. Every number here is a floor, never a total: a refusal
the harness did not mark, and a write whose success line was reworded, are both
invisible to it. So a clean run licenses nothing — it says these classes are
clean, and the reader at the close still owes the rest of the inventory.

IT ALSO OVERCOUNTS, in two known shapes. A write whose output was FILTERED: a
claim piped into `tail -1` prints its success line into that pipe, and what the
transcript records is what survived the filter. And a write whose output was
TRUNCATED: past a few dozen KB the harness saves the whole thing to
`<session dir>/tool-results/<id>.txt` and leaves `Preview (first 2KB)` in the
transcript, so a success line below that cut is not in the file this reads. Both
rows are still worth printing — the reader checks the queue and closes the
question in one command — but they are the class to expect a false positive
from, and the reason this class reports rows rather than a verdict.

IT READS THE AGENTS THIS SESSION DISPATCHED. Their transcripts are separate
files, as `tk-context` documents, and their refusals are this session's: a hook
that turned a dispatched agent away refused work this session asked for, and the
close is the only place anybody reads it. So the session's own jsonl and every
`<session dir>/subagents/**/*.jsonl` beside it are one reading. Every record in
an agent's file is marked `isSidechain`, and the PARENT file's sidechain records
— copies of the same lines — are skipped there so no row is printed twice.

A READING THAT FOUND NO RECORD IS NOT A CLEAN SESSION. An empty file, a file of
garbage and a file whose every record was filtered out all exit 2, for the reason
a missing file does: "nothing was read" and "nothing was refused" are opposite
facts, and this is the seam where a file that OPENED could report the first as
the second.

Usage:  tk-errors [--limit N] [--session ID] [--transcript PATH]
Exit:   0 the reading happened · 2 nothing was read · 64 bad usage.

THE EXIT CODE SAYS WHETHER IT READ, NEVER WHAT IT FOUND. The verdict is the
headline, and this command is run BY the close, through the Bash tool, which
records a non-zero exit as `is_error`. Exiting 1 on "I found something" made
every honest run a refusal in the NEXT session's class 1 — the reader
manufacturing the very signal it exists to count. 0 and 2 stay distinct for the
original reason: "the session was clean" and "the reading never happened" are
opposite facts, and a seam that conflates them reports the second as the first.
"""

import argparse
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import sys
from typing import NamedTuple

EXIT_READ = 0                         # the reading happened; the headline judges
EXIT_NO_TRANSCRIPT = 2
EXIT_USAGE = 64                       # sysexits.h EX_USAGE; argparse's own 2 collides

# The subcommands of `tk-queue` that CHANGE the queue, each against the success
# line it prints. Every pattern below is the tail of a real `print` in
# `tk/bin/tk-queue`; `LITERALS` names the substring of that f-string a test
# greps for, so a reworded line fails the suite instead of going quiet here.
#
# Two-shape entries are not redundancy: `release` on an unclaimed item, `done`
# on an item already in the done-log and `bump` on an item already at the top
# are honest no-ops that tk-queue reports in their own words, and reading them
# as failures would report a write that did happen as missing.
#
# The table is the whole class: a writing subcommand missing from it is a write
# nobody checks. `handoff`, `bump` and `migrate` were missing, and each writes a
# file — the briefing, the queue's order, the done-log.
SUCCESS = {
    "add": (r"^added T\S*: ",),
    "edit": (r"^\S+ updated$",),
    "claim": (r"^\S+ claimed by \S+",),
    "release": (r"^\S+ released — ", r"^\S+ carries no claim — nothing to release$"),
    "done": (r"^\S+ → done-log as ", r"^\S+ → out of the queue;"),
    "cancel": (r"^\S+ → done-log as ", r"^\S+ → out of the queue;"),
    "handoff": (r"^(?:re)?wrote \S*handoff-T\d+\.md$",),
    "bump": (r"^\S+ → top of the queue$",
             r"^\S+ is already at the top of the queue$"),
    "migrate": (r"^\d+ \[x\] item\(s\) → done-log;",),
}
# What `test_tk_errors.py` requires to still be a literal in `tk-queue`, one per
# pattern above. Derived from the same reading and kept beside it so the two
# cannot drift apart unnoticed.
LITERALS = {
    "add": ('print(f"added T{iid:03d}',),
    "edit": ('print(f"{label} updated")',),
    "claim": ('print(f"{label} claimed by ',),
    "release": ('print(f"{label} released — ',
                'carries no claim — nothing to release'),
    "done": ('→ done-log as ', '→ out of the queue;'),
    "cancel": ('→ done-log as ', '→ out of the queue;'),
    "handoff": ('print(("rewrote " if existed else "wrote ") + path)',),
    "bump": ('print(f"{label} → top of the queue")',
             'print(f"{label} is already at the top of the queue")'),
    "migrate": ('[x] item(s) → done-log;',),
}

QUEUE_BIN = "tk-queue"
# `--dir` is the one option that takes a value and may stand BEFORE the
# subcommand (`tk-queue --dir X add ...`). Skipping the flag without skipping its
# value would read the path as the subcommand.
VALUE_FLAGS = ("--dir",)
# Commands that READ their argument rather than run it. A briefing that quotes an
# invocation is not one, and unquoted it looks exactly like one.
READERS = ("echo", "printf", "grep", "rg", "cat", "head", "tail", "less",
           "man", "which", "type", "comm", "diff")
# Where one invocation ends inside a compound command. `tokenize` below is what
# makes these reachable: the plain splitter keeps `CHORE;` in one word, and every
# boundary written without a space around it would be invisible here. A newline
# is one of them — the commonest, in a multi-line Bash call — and the only one
# the shell also treats as whitespace.
SEPARATORS = (";", "|", "||", "&", "&&", "\n")
# What `shlex` is told to tokenize on its own; its own default is `();<>|&`.
PUNCTUATION = "();<>|&\n"
# A subcommand invoked with `--help` writes nothing. Counted as a write it reports
# a mismatch on every session that looked the flags up — measured, 3 of 9 rows on
# the first real transcript this ran against.
HELP_FLAGS = ("--help", "-h")
# A here-document opener, quoted (`<<'EOF'`) or bare (`<< EOF`), `<<-` included.
# `<<<` is a here-STRING and does not match: the character after `<<` is not a
# word character there.
HEREDOC_OPEN = re.compile(r"<<-?\s*(['\"]?)(?P<word>[A-Za-z_][\w.-]*)\1")
# The subcommand slot of a command the tokenizer refused. Never a real one: what
# it invoked cannot be known without a shell, and guessing is what class 3 exists
# to stop.
UNPARSED = "?"

DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20                    # rows per class before the tail is summarised
MESSAGE_CHARS = 160                   # of a refusal message; the whole is in the jsonl


class Refusal(NamedTuple):
    """One tool call the harness answered with `is_error: true`."""

    stamp: str
    tool: str
    detail: str
    message: str


class Unconfirmed(NamedTuple):
    """One `tk-queue` write whose success line never appeared."""

    stamp: str
    sub: str
    command: str
    why: str


class Unparsed(NamedTuple):
    """One command naming `tk-queue` that the tokenizer refused."""

    stamp: str
    command: str
    why: str


def plain(value):
    """A value from outside this process, safe to print to a terminal.

    Everything this command prints was read out of a jsonl or handed in on the
    command line: a session id, a path, a timestamp, a shell command somebody
    typed, and an error message written by a hook. Control sequences are stripped
    where the value ENTERS the output rather than trusted at their source — the
    sibling channel where this house measured the defect carried the escape in a
    directory NAME.
    """
    return re.sub(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f]", "?", str(value))


def no_transcript(msg):
    """Stop with code 2, and say that nothing was read.

    Said aloud because the alternative reading is the dangerous one: a reader
    that took silence for cleanliness would report an unread session as a session
    with nothing wrong in it.
    """
    print(f"tk-errors: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    print("tk-errors: nothing was read — this is not a clean session, it is an "
          "unread one", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(EXIT_NO_TRANSCRIPT)


def find_transcript(session_id):
    """The jsonl for one session id, under any project directory.

    The id is ESCAPED before it reaches the glob: unescaped, `--session '*'`
    matches a stranger's transcript, and this command would report another
    session's refusals as this one's. Two files for one id is refused rather than
    resolved, exactly as `tk-context` refuses it — sorting picks a winner in
    silence and the loser is as likely to be the live session.
    """
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    hits = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, "*", f"{glob.escape(session_id)}.jsonl")))
    if not hits:
        no_transcript(f"no transcript for session {plain(session_id)} under {root}")
    if len(hits) > 1:
        no_transcript("two transcripts carry this session id: "
                      + ", ".join(plain(h) for h in hits))
    return hits[0]


def subagent_transcripts(path):
    """The jsonls of the agents this session dispatched, beside its own file.

    The harness writes them under `<transcript without .jsonl>/subagents/`, one
    per agent, one level deeper for an agent a Workflow ran — hence the
    recursive glob. The directory is escaped before it reaches that glob, for
    the reason `find_transcript` escapes the session id.
    """
    base = re.sub(r"\.jsonl$", "", path)
    return sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(glob.escape(base), "subagents",
                                         "**", "*.jsonl"), recursive=True))


def blocks(record):
    """The content blocks of a record, or an empty list.

    `message.content` is a list of blocks on the records that carry tool traffic
    and a bare string on plain text turns, so the type is checked rather than
    assumed: indexing a string here yields characters and every `.get` below
    would raise on the first one.
    """
    content = (record.get("message") or {}).get("content")
    return [b for b in content if isinstance(b, dict)] if isinstance(content, list) else []


def block_text(block):
    """A tool_result's payload as text, whichever of its two shapes it took.

    The harness writes a string for an ordinary result and a list of typed blocks
    when the tool returned structured content. A reader that handled only the
    string would score every structured result as "printed no success line".
    """
    content = block.get("content")
    if isinstance(content, str):
        return content
    if isinstance(content, list):
        return "\n".join(part.get("text") or "" for part in content
                         if isinstance(part, dict))
    return ""


def strip_heredocs(command):
    """The command without the BODIES of its here-documents.

    A heredoc body is data the shell hands to another program — a file being
    written, a commit message, a briefing — and this house writes all three with
    `tk-queue` invocations quoted inside them. Read as command text they are
    invocations that never ran: on one real transcript the body of a `git commit
    -F -` heredoc reported a `done` that was a line in the commit message.

    The cost, stated rather than hidden: a command actually RUN from a heredoc
    (`bash <<EOF`) is invisible to this reader. That is the floor this file
    already declares, and it is the cheaper of the two errors — a missed row
    sends nobody anywhere, and an invented one is why a report stops being read.
    """
    kept, awaiting = [], []
    for line in command.split("\n"):
        if awaiting:
            if line.strip() == awaiting[0]:
                awaiting.pop(0)       # `<<-` allows the terminator to be indented
            continue
        kept.append(line)
        awaiting = [found.group("word") for found in HEREDOC_OPEN.finditer(line)]
    return "\n".join(kept)


def tokenize(command):
    """The command split as the shell splits it, punctuation and all.

    `shlex.split` breaks on WHITESPACE and never on a shell metacharacter, so
    `add X; tk-queue done --help` hands back `X;` as one word and the boundary
    between the two invocations is not in the token list at all. Unspaced `;` is
    how this house writes a batched command, so that was the common shape, not
    the exotic one. `punctuation_chars` gives `();<>|&` tokens of their own while
    leaving a metacharacter inside quotes part of its word — which is the
    distinction `SEPARATORS` needs and the plain splitter cannot make.

    A NEWLINE is one of those boundaries, and the default lexer eats it as
    whitespace: `<write>\ndf -h /` left the `-h` of an unrelated later command
    inside the write's segment, which suppressed the write. So `\n` is taken out
    of `whitespace` and put into `punctuation_chars`, where it earns a token of
    its own and stays part of the word when it falls inside quotes.

    Raises `ValueError` on quoting it cannot close, exactly as `shlex.split`
    does; the caller reports that command as unparsed.
    """
    lexer = shlex.shlex(command, posix=True, punctuation_chars=PUNCTUATION)
    lexer.whitespace = " \t\r"            # `\n` is a separator here, not a space
    lexer.whitespace_split = True         # words, not the lexer's default atoms
    # A run of newlines lexes as ONE token; collapsed so `SEPARATORS` can ask
    # plain membership, since two blank lines separate two commands as one does.
    return ["\n" if token and not token.strip("\n") else token
            for token in lexer]           # `commenters` is already `#` by default


def queue_writes(command):
    """The writing `tk-queue` subcommands this shell command invokes, in order.

    Returns a list of (subcommand, note) pairs, `note` being empty on a clean
    reading. A command the tokenizer refuses yields ONE pair, in the `UNPARSED`
    slot, which the caller reports as class 3. It does NOT yield a subcommand: a
    regex over the raw text is how the earlier version guessed, and what it read
    were the English words inside heredocs the shell had run fine.
    """
    if QUEUE_BIN not in command:
        # The cheap gate, and a correctness one: without it the tokenizer's
        # failure below would report every unbalanced quote in the session as an
        # unconfirmed queue write, which is a wrong number in the class this
        # command exists to count.
        return []
    try:
        tokens = tokenize(strip_heredocs(command))
    except ValueError as exc:
        return [(UNPARSED, f"the command does not tokenize ({exc})")]

    out = []
    for index, token in enumerate(tokens):
        if os.path.basename(token) != QUEUE_BIN:
            continue
        if index and os.path.basename(tokens[index - 1]) in READERS:
            continue                  # the command reads this name, it does not run it
        rest = tokens[index + 1:]
        position = 0
        while position < len(rest) and rest[position].startswith("-"):
            flag = rest[position]
            position += 2 if flag in VALUE_FLAGS else 1
        if position < len(rest) and rest[position] in SUCCESS:
            segment = []
            for later in rest[position + 1:]:
                # The binary's own name ends the segment too: a trailing `#`
                # comment swallows the newline after it, leaving two invocations
                # with no separator token between them, and the `--help` of the
                # SECOND would otherwise suppress THIS write.
                if later in SEPARATORS or os.path.basename(later) == QUEUE_BIN:
                    break
                segment.append(later)
            if not any(flag in segment for flag in HELP_FLAGS):
                out.append((rest[position], ""))
    return out


def success_count(text, sub):
    """How many success lines for `sub` this call printed.

    Line by line and anchored: the shapes start at the item's label, and an
    unanchored search would find one inside a quoted echo of an earlier run — the
    briefings this house writes quote queue output verbatim.
    """
    patterns = SUCCESS[sub]
    return sum(1 for line in text.splitlines()
               if any(re.search(p, line.strip()) for p in patterns))


def walk(path):
    """(refusals, unconfirmed writes) from this session's transcripts, oldest first.

    ONE READING OVER SEVERAL FILES: the session's own jsonl, then the transcript
    of every agent it dispatched. The parent's `isSidechain` records are skipped
    — the agent's own file holds the same lines, and reading both would print
    every dispatched refusal twice. A key is the (file, tool_use_id) pair rather
    than the id alone, so no agent's output can confirm another's write.

    Read line by line: a long session's transcript reaches megabytes and only two
    kinds of block are wanted. Malformed lines are skipped rather than fatal — the
    file is appended to live, so its last line can be half-written, and a session
    asking what it was refused is by definition still running.

    The two passes are one walk over an append-only file: a `tool_use` is always
    written before its `tool_result`, so the calls are known by the time the
    results arrive. A call whose result never arrives keeps an empty text and is
    reported unconfirmed, which is what it is — the write was invoked and nothing
    came back to say it landed.

    Zero records CONSIDERED stops with code 2. The count is of what survived the
    reading, not of what the file held: a transcript that opened and yielded
    nothing is unread, and reporting it as clean is the one lie this command
    cannot afford.
    """
    calls = {}                        # (file, tool_use_id) -> (stamp, tool, input)
    refusals = []
    pending = []                      # (stamp, sub, command, note, key)
    results = {}                      # (file, tool_use_id) -> the text it returned
    considered = 0

    for source in [path] + subagent_transcripts(path):
        try:
            handle = open(source, errors="replace")
        except OSError as exc:
            if source == path:
                no_transcript(f"cannot read {path}: {exc}")
            print(f"tk-errors: skipped {plain(source)}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        with handle:
            for line in handle:
                try:
                    record = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if source == path and record.get("isSidechain"):
                    continue          # the agent's own file carries this line
                considered += 1
                stamp = record.get("timestamp") or ""
                for block in blocks(record):
                    kind = block.get("type")
                    if kind == "tool_use":
                        key = (source, block.get("id"))
                        calls[key] = (stamp, block.get("name") or "?",
                                      block.get("input") or {})
                        command = (block.get("input") or {}).get("command")
                        if isinstance(command, str):
                            for sub, note in queue_writes(command):
                                pending.append((stamp, sub, command, note, key))
                    elif kind == "tool_result":
                        key = (source, block.get("tool_use_id"))
                        text = block_text(block)
                        results[key] = results.get(key, "") + text
                        if block.get("is_error"):
                            called = calls.get(key, (stamp, "?", {}))
                            refusals.append(Refusal(called[0] or stamp, called[1],
                                                    call_detail(called[2]), text))

    if not considered:
        no_transcript(f"{plain(path)} yielded no record of this session")

    unconfirmed, unparsed = [], []
    spent = {}                        # (call, subcommand) -> lines already claimed
    for stamp, sub, command, note, key in pending:
        if sub == UNPARSED:
            unparsed.append(Unparsed(stamp, command, note))
            continue
        text = results.get(key)
        if text is None:
            unconfirmed.append(Unconfirmed(stamp, sub, command,
                                           "the call returned nothing"))
            continue
        # One success line confirms ONE invocation. A presence check would let
        # the first line answer for every `add` in a batched command, and a
        # batch whose second half failed is the shape that fails.
        claimed = spent.get((key, sub), 0)
        printed = success_count(text, sub)
        if claimed < printed:
            spent[(key, sub)] = claimed + 1
            continue
        unconfirmed.append(Unconfirmed(
            stamp, sub, command,
            f"no `{sub}` success line in the output" if not printed else
            f"{printed} `{sub}` success line(s) for {claimed + 1} "
            f"invocation(s) in this call"))
    return refusals, unconfirmed, unparsed


def call_detail(payload):
    """The one field that says WHICH call this was, for the row.

    A refusal row naming only the tool is unactionable — a session makes hundreds
    of Bash calls — so the command, the path or the pattern goes in the row and
    the rest of the input stays in the jsonl.
    """
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return ""
    for key in ("command", "file_path", "path", "pattern", "url", "prompt"):
        value = payload.get(key)
        if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip():
            return value
    return ""


def clip(text, chars):
    """One line of at most `chars`, with the cut declared.

    Never a silent truncation: a message cut at a word boundary reads like the
    whole message, and the reader acts on half a sentence without knowing it.
    """
    folded = plain(re.sub(r"\s+", " ", str(text))).strip()
    return folded if len(folded) <= chars else folded[:chars] + " […cut]"


def report(refusals, unconfirmed, unparsed, limit):
    """The three classes, headline first.

    The headline is one line and the stable half of this contract: a seam that
    greps or pipes this command reads it, and the rows below are for the human
    or the agent that then goes looking. Every class reaches it, including the
    advisory one — a count printed only below the rows is a count nobody reads.
    """
    print(f"{len(refusals)} refused tool call(s), "
          f"{len(unconfirmed)} unconfirmed tk-queue write(s), "
          f"{len(unparsed)} command(s) not parsed")

    for title, rows, render in (
            ("refused", refusals,
             lambda r: f"  {plain(r.stamp)[:19] or '?':19}  {plain(r.tool):12} "
                       f"{clip(r.message, MESSAGE_CHARS)}\n"
                       f"  {'':19}  {'':12} call: {clip(r.detail, MESSAGE_CHARS)}"),
            ("unconfirmed tk-queue writes", unconfirmed,
             lambda r: f"  {plain(r.stamp)[:19] or '?':19}  {plain(r.sub):12} "
                       f"{plain(r.why)}\n"
                       f"  {'':19}  {'':12} call: {clip(r.command, MESSAGE_CHARS)}"),
            ("commands not parsed (advisory — most are heredocs that ran)",
             unparsed,
             lambda r: f"  {plain(r.stamp)[:19] or '?':19}  {plain(r.why)}\n"
                       f"  {'':19}  call: {clip(r.command, MESSAGE_CHARS)}")):
        if not rows:
            continue
        print(f"\n{title}:")
        for row in rows[:limit]:
            print(render(row))
        if len(rows) > limit:
            print(f"  … {len(rows) - limit} more, in the transcript")


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    """Usage errors exit 64, not argparse's 2.

    2 is this command's "there is no transcript to read". A mistyped flag
    arriving at a seam as that message is the one confusion the exit codes exist
    to prevent, and it is the same choice `tk-context` makes for the same reason.
    """

    def error(self, message):
        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)
        print(f"{self.prog}: error: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(EXIT_USAGE)


def main(argv=None):
    parser = Parser(
        description="list what this session was refused, from its own transcript")
    parser.add_argument("--limit", type=int, default=DEFAULT_LIMIT,
                        help=f"rows per class before the tail is summarised "
                             f"(default: {DEFAULT_LIMIT})")
    parser.add_argument("--session", default=os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID"),
                        help="session id (default: $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)")
    parser.add_argument("--transcript",
                        help="read this jsonl instead of resolving the session id")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.limit < 1:
        parser.error("--limit must be at least 1")

    path = args.transcript
    if not path:
        if not args.session:
            no_transcript("no CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID in the environment")
        path = find_transcript(args.session)

    refusals, unconfirmed, unparsed = walk(path)
    report(refusals, unconfirmed, unparsed, args.limit)
    return EXIT_READ


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
